    "['vsi [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols vpls no-tunnel-services\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, a VSI is configured under a routing-instance with type vpls.\\n2. The \"no-tunnel-services\" statement is added as Juniper typically requires explicit configuration for VPLS without MPLS tunnels (though in real scenarios, you\\'d configure proper LDP or BGP signaling). \\n3. Additional parameters like RD, VPN targets, and interfaces would need separate commands to fully match the Huawei configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols vpls no-tunnel-services', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols vpls no-tunnel-services', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols vpls no-tunnel-services\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, a VSI is configured under a routing-instance with type vpls.\\n2. The \"no-tunnel-services\" statement is added as Juniper typically requires explicit configuration for VPLS without MPLS tunnels (though in real scenarios, you\\'d configure proper LDP or BGP signaling). \\n3. Additional parameters like RD, VPN targets, and interfaces would need separate commands to fully match the Huawei configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols vpls no-tunnel-services'}]]": 1,
    "['pwsignal bgp', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of `pwsignal bgp`:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 interface ge-1/0/0.1\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 remote-site-id 2\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 no-control-word\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 ignore-mtu-mismatch\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, L2VPN configuration is done under routing-instances (equivalent to VSI in Huawei)\\n2. The `pwsignal bgp` command in Huawei indicates BGP signaling for the pseudowire, which in Juniper is configured through L2VPN protocol settings\\n3. The Juniper commands establish:\\n   - L2VPN encapsulation type\\n   - Local site interface\\n   - Remote site identifier\\n   - Optional parameters like control-word and MTU handling\\n\\nNote: The exact remote-site-id would need to match the peer configuration, and additional BGP signaling parameters might be needed in the BGP configuration depending on the full setup.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 interface ge-1/0/0.1', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of `pwsignal bgp`:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 interface ge-1/0/0.1\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 remote-site-id 2\\nset routing-instances v1 protocol