-id would need to match the peer configuration, and additional BGP signaling parameters might be needed in the BGP configuration depending on the full setup.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 remote-site-id 2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 no-control-word', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of `pwsignal bgp`:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 interface ge-1/0/0.1\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 remote-site-id 2\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 no-control-word\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 ignore-mtu-mismatch\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, L2VPN configuration is done under routing-instances (equivalent to VSI in Huawei)\\n2. The `pwsignal bgp` command in Huawei indicates BGP signaling for the pseudowire, which in Juniper is configured through L2VPN protocol settings\\n3. The Juniper commands establish:\\n   - L2VPN encapsulation type\\n   - Local site interface\\n   - Remote site identifier\\n   - Optional parameters like control-word and MTU handling\\n\\nNote: The exact remote-site-id would need to match the peer configuration, and additional BGP signaling parameters might be needed in the BGP configuration depending on the full setup.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 no-control-word'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 ignore-mtu-mismatch', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of `pwsignal bgp`:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 interface ge-1/0/0.1\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 remote-site-id 2\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 no-control-word\\nset routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 ignore-mtu-mismatch\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, L2VPN configuration is done under routing-instances (equivalent to VSI in Huawei)\\n2. The `pwsignal bgp` command in Huawei indicates BGP signaling for the pseudowire, which in Juniper is configured through L2VPN protocol settings\\n3. The Juniper commands establish:\\n   - L2VPN encapsulation type\\n   - Local site interface\\n   - Remote site identifier\\n   - Optional parameters like control-word and MTU handling\\n\\nNote: The exact remote-site-id would need to match the peer configuration, and additional BGP signaling parameters might be needed in the BGP configuration depending on the full setup.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn site 1 ignore-mtu-mismatch'}]]": 1,
    "['route-distinguisher [parameter1]:[parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1;', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nroute-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1;\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the command is similar but requires a semicolon (;) at the end. The RD format remains the same (IP:value or ASN:value).', 'trans_template': 'route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1;'}]]": 1,
    "['vpn-target [parameter1]:[parameter2] import-extcommunity', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI `vpn-target 100:1 import-extcommunity` command:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1\\nset routing-instances v1 vrf-target import target:100:1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, the VSI (Virtual Switch Instance) would typically be configured under a routing-instance of type \"vpls\"\\n2. I included the route-distinguisher line as it\\'s typically paired with vrf-target in Juniper configurations\\n3. Juniper uses \"vrf-target\" instead of \"vpn-target\" and the format is slightly different with \"target:\" prefix', 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 vrf-target import target:100:1', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI `vpn-target 100:1 import-extcommunity` command:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1\\nset routing-instances v1 vrf-target import target:100:1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, the VSI (Virtual Switch Instance) would typically be configured under a routing-instance of type \"vpls\"\\n2. I included the route-distinguisher line as it\\'s typically paired with vrf-target in Juniper configurations\\n3. Juniper uses \"vrf-target\" instead of \"vpn-target\" and the format is slightly different with \"target:\" prefix', 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 vrf-target import target:100:1'}]]": 1,
    "['vpn-target [parameter1]:[parameter2] export-extcommunity', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei `vpn-target 100:1 export-extcommunity` command:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1\\nset routing-instances v1 vrf-target target:100:1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses `vrf-target` instead of `vpn-target`\\n2. The format changes from `100:1` to `target:100:1`\\n3. Juniper doesn't have separate import/export commands - the same `vrf-target` is used for both unless explicitly configured otherwise\\n4. I included the route-distinguisher as it's typically configured together with vrf-target in Juniper\\n5. The instance name `v1` is preserved from the original VSI configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 vrf-target target:100:1', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'set routing-instances v1 protocols l2vpn encapsulation-type ethernet'], 'root': 'set routing-instances v1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei `vpn-target 100:1 export-extcommunity` command:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances v1 route-distinguisher 10.10.3.4:1\\nset routing-instances v1 vrf-target target:100:1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses `vrf-target` instead of `vpn-target`\\n2. The format changes from `100:1` to `target:100:1`\\n3. Juniper doesn't have separate import/export commands - the same `vrf-target` is used for both unless explicitly configured otherwise\\n4. I included the route-distinguisher as it's typically configured together