    "['l2vpn-ad-family', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'family l2vpn', 'parent_command': ['set routing-options autonomous-system [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set routing-options autonomous-system [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration for the HUAWEI `l2vpn-ad-family` command:\\n\\n##\\nfamily l2vpn\\n##\\n\\nIn Juniper, the `family l2vpn` is used to enable Layer 2 VPN address family in BGP configuration, which is equivalent to HUAWEI's `l2vpn-ad-family`. This is typically configured under the BGP neighbor or group configuration context.\", 'trans_template': 'family l2vpn'}]]": 1,
    "['policy vpn-target', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances v1 vrf-target target:100:1', 'parent_command': ['set routing-options autonomous-system [parameter1]', 'family l2vpn'], 'root': 'set routing-options autonomous-system [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset routing-instances v1 vrf-target target:100:1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei `policy vpn-target` command in the BGP L2VPN AD family context enforces VPN route filtering based on the configured VPN targets (RTs). In Juniper, this is typically implemented using the `vrf-target` statement under the routing instance configuration.\\n\\nThe equivalent Juniper command sets the import/export route targets for the VPLS instance (v1 in this case), using the same target community 100:1 that was specified in the Huawei configuration's `vpn-target` statements.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances v1 vrf-target target:100:1'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-selector [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement s1 then load-balance per-packet', 'parent_command': ['set routing-options autonomous-system [parameter1]', 'family l2vpn'], 'root': 'set routing-options autonomous-system [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent translation for the `tunnel-selector s1` command:\\n\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement s1 then load-balance per-packet\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, tunnel selection policies are typically implemented using route policies (`policy-statement`) with load-balancing directives.\\n- The Huawei `tunnel-selector` is roughly equivalent to Juniper's `policy-statement` for tunnel selection purposes.\\n- The `per-packet` load balancing mimics the behavior specified in the Huawei configuration where the tunnel policy `p1` uses `load-balance-number 1`.\\n- Note that this is a simplified translation - a complete Juniper implementation might require additional context about the tunnel interfaces and protocols being used.\", 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement s1 then load-balance per-packet'}]]": 1,
